{
  "summary": "Suppresses self-advertisement of peer-reported addresses on outbound connections made through a proxy. In `GetLocalAddrForPeer`, the node skips using the address observed by an outbound peer if a proxy is configured for that network, preventing proxy exit IPs from being gossiped. Operators with reachable proxies can still advertise them using `-externalip`.",
  "problem": "Nodes making outbound clearnet connections through a SOCKS5 proxy (such as Tor) receive the proxy exit node's IP address in the peer's view of them. If address discovery remains enabled, the node self-advertises this proxy exit IP, polluting peers' address managers with unreachable addresses.",
  "evidence": [
    "The author reports that '55-76% of all tor exit nodes were present in various addrman snapshots I checked (my node and the ones from b10c’s peer.observer) from now and few years ago' (PR description)."
  ],
  "dependencies": {
    "depends_on": [],
    "enables": []
  },
  "scope_notes": "",
  "changed_since_previous": "The earlier documentation update for Tor configuration parameters is no longer present; the diff now only contains the net logic change, `ResetProxy()` test helper, unit tests, and release notes.",
  "card": "PR 35578 stops nodes from self-advertising peer-observed IP addresses on outbound connections routed through a proxy like Tor. Currently, outbound peers see the proxy or Tor exit node IP and report it back, causing nodes to gossip unreachable proxy IPs across the P2P network. Addrman snapshots cited by the author showed 55-76% of all Tor exit nodes present in address tables due to this behavior. Operators running reachable proxies can still explicitly advertise them using -externalip.",
